     1	# NPM shim integrity manifest
     2	
     3	> **Integrity contract for the `ceo-orchestration` npm shim.** This file
     4	> states **no** version of its own: `VERSION` at the repo root is the single
     5	> authority, mirrored into `npm/package.json` by the release bump driver
     6	> (`.claude/scripts/local/_release_bump_sites.py`) — a second copy here would
     7	> be a literal no oracle watches, which is exactly how it went stale before.
     8	> Publishing stays gated — `npm-publish.yml` holds all `v*-rc.*` tags and
     9	> requires manual approval on GA tags via `environment: production-npm`.
    10	> **Which controls run, and where, is stated in exactly one place: the
    11	> Contract table below.** This prose deliberately makes no mechanism claim of
    12	> its own — a claim written here would be answerable to nothing, which is how
    13	> this file came to advertise a tarball checksum that no workflow ever
    14	> produced. The table's `Status` column and its machine-checked
    15	> `Where enforced` cells are the answer; §Not yet automated names the gaps.
    16	> In summary, and stated as absences: there is **no** per-tarball SHA-256
    17	> manifest anywhere in the publish pipeline (no workflow materialises the
    18	> `.tgz` — the packlist gate runs `npm pack --dry-run`, which writes nothing
    19	> — so nothing hashes it), and neither the GPG detached signature nor the
    20	> reproducible build is automated.
    21	
    22	## Contract
    23	
    24	The rows below are the contract for every release tarball.
    25	
    26	`Status` is a **closed set** — `enforced`, `deferred`, `operator`. Anything
    27	else is a test failure, so a row cannot acquire a comfortable new adjective.
    28	`Where enforced` is **machine-checked** for `enforced` rows
    29	(`test_integrity_contract_rows_name_a_live_step` in
    30	`.claude/scripts/tests/test_release_bump_sites.py`): the cell must name a
    31	workflow in backticks that exists, followed by `step "<name>"` matching a
    32	`- name:` in that YAML by **exact equality** — never substring. A row that
    33	claims enforcement without a live step is the failure mode this table
    34	shipped with, and it now goes red.
    35	
    36	| Control | Value / mechanism | Status | Where enforced |
    37	|---|---|---|---|
    38	| Zero runtime dependencies | `Object.keys(dependencies).length === 0` | enforced | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Verify zero runtime dependencies" |
    39	| VERSION parity | `VERSION` == `npm/package.json.version` == tag `v<version>` | enforced | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Verify VERSION matches tag" + `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Verify npm/package.json version matches VERSION" |
    40	| Packlist hygiene | no numbered `PLAN-N` artifacts, framework test suites, eval corpora or red-team corpus in the tarball — the SHIPPED exceptions are `.claude/policies/fixtures/` (policy-as-code inputs), `templates/oidc-proxy/tests/` (template ships with its tests) and the plan SCHEMA/example docs | enforced | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Packlist gate (PLAN-152 tarball-02)" + `.github/workflows/validate.yml` step "npm packlist gate (no tests/fixtures/eval/red-team-corpus/PLAN-N)" |
    41	| `install.sh` self-SHA trailer | `# CEO-INSTALL-SHA256:` stamped over the staged installer; the installer re-hashes its own body at install time and fails closed on mismatch | enforced | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Populate install.sh self-SHA trailer (P0-15, PLAN-045)" |
    42	| Tag/SHA binding at publish | the remote tag must still point at the SHA this run built | enforced | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Assert remote tag still points at this run's SHA" |
    43	| SLSA Level-2 provenance | `npm publish --provenance` (Sigstore-attested via OIDC) | enforced | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Publish (Trusted Publishing — OIDC)" |
    44	| SHA-256 tarball manifest | `sha256sum` over the published `.tgz`, plus a detached `<tarball>.sha256` | deferred | nothing — see §Not yet automated |
    45	| Reproducible build | `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` pinned to the VERSION tag commit date | deferred | nothing — see §Not yet automated |
    46	| GPG detached signature | RFC 4880 signature over tarball | operator | a human, by hand, if at all — see §Not yet automated |
    47	
    48	## SHA-256 tarball manifest — what actually exists
    49	
    50	`npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` is in the tree, and it is easy to read it as a shipped
    51	guarantee. It is not one:
    52	
    53	- It is written by **`scripts/install-npm.sh`**, a local build helper a
    54	  maintainer runs on a workstation. No workflow invokes it, and no workflow
    55	  appends to the file on tag push.
    56	- It therefore records whatever tarball was last built locally, which lags
    57	  the published package.
    58	- It does **not** travel inside the package: `SHA256SUMS.txt` is absent from
    59	  the `files:` array in `npm/package.json`, so it is not in the tarball a
    60	  consumer installs.
    61	
    62	Earlier revisions of this document published a consumer recipe that ran
    63	`sha256sum -c` over that manifest from inside the installed package. The
    64	recipe has been **removed, not annotated**: it could never run, for any
    65	version, because the file it reads is not in the package. A caveated
    66	impossible recipe is still an impossible recipe, and it is not reproduced
    67	here — a reader who finds a command in a doc will run it.
    68	
    69	What a consumer can actually verify is the provenance attestation — see
    70	§SLSA Level-2 provenance below. For the bash install path, the
    71	`install.sh.sha256` release asset is the checksum that exists; `SECURITY.md`
    72	§How to verify what you install states its scope and its limits.
    73	
    74	## Signing keys
    75	
    76	The Owner's public key is committed in-repo at `.claude/trust/owner.asc`.
    77	What it signs today is **release tags**. That gate lives outside this
    78	contract and is documented — with its exact scope — in `SECURITY.md`
    79	§How to verify what you install, which is its authority; this file does not
    80	restate it. Verify locally with
    81	`gpg --import .claude/trust/owner.asc && git tag --verify vX.Y.Z`.
    82	
    83	There is **no** separate npm signing key. Two distribution points were
    84	claimed here and neither exists: a fingerprint in `docs/rotation-log.md`
    85	(that log covers API-key rotation — its NPM entry records the retirement of
    86	`NPM_TOKEN` in favour of OIDC, and it publishes no key material), and a
    87	public key served under a `.well-known/` path (this project serves no such
    88	path at all). The detached-signature row above is `operator` for that reason:
    89	nothing signs a tarball today.
    90	
    91	## SLSA Level-2 provenance
    92	
    93	`npm publish --provenance` attaches a Sigstore-signed attestation declaring:
    94	- Source repository (git URL)
    95	- Commit SHA
    96	- Workflow file that built the tarball
    97	- Builder (GitHub Actions runner identity)
    98	
    99	Consumers inspect via one of two routes. `npm audit signatures` reads the
   100	dependency tree of the CURRENT project and takes no package-selecting
   101	argument (a trailing package name is ignored), so it reaches this package
   102	only when this package is a dependency of the project you run it in. On a
   103	global install it exits with `EAUDITGLOBAL`, whose message states that
   104	global packages are not supported.
   105	
   106	Route A — global install (`npm install -g`) or `npx`: open the
   107	"Provenance" panel on <https://www.npmjs.com/package/ceo-orchestration>
   108	and confirm the source repository shown there is
   109	`Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration`.
   110	
   111	Route B — audit locally, by making the package a dependency of a
   112	throwaway project:
   113	
   114	```bash
   115	mkdir /tmp/verify-ceo && cd /tmp/verify-ceo && npm init -y
   116	npm install ceo-orchestration
   117	npm audit signatures   # audits THIS project's tree, which now holds it
   118	```
   119	
   120	## Reproducible-build spec
   121	
   122	**Status: specification only — no workflow implements any part of it.**
   123	Inputs:
   124	- `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH = <VERSION tag creator-date, unix-epoch>`
   125	- Node 20.x (`npm-publish.yml` step "Setup Node 20")
   126	- No `npm install` for the bundle itself (zero runtime deps)
   127	
   128	Intended output: byte-identical tarball across any ubuntu-latest GitHub
   129	Actions runner with the same inputs. No workflow performs the rebuild or the
   130	comparison, and no workflow sets `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` at all, so a deviation
   131	would go unnoticed.
   132	
   133	## CI verification (what the packlist gate does, and does not, prove)
   134	
   135	Both `npm-publish.yml` and `validate.yml` run a packlist gate over
   136	`npm pack --dry-run --json`, and its subject is the **file list** the tarball
   137	would contain (no numbered `PLAN-N` artifacts, framework test suites, eval
   138	corpora or red-team corpus; `.claude/policies/fixtures/`,
   139	`templates/oidc-proxy/tests/` and the plan SCHEMA/example docs SHIP by
   140	design). `--dry-run`
   141	writes no archive, so the gate proves nothing about tarball **bytes** — no
   142	checksum, no signature, no reproducibility. Tarball hashing and signing are
   143	the un-automated controls above; provenance is the one byte-level attestation
   144	that ships.
   145	
   146	## Not yet automated (release-operator or out of scope)
   147	
   148	- **SHA-256 tarball manifest** — no workflow materialises the `.tgz`, so
   149	  none can hash it; `npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` is a local-build artefact and is not
   150	  shipped inside the package.
   151	- **GPG detached signature** would be a manual release-operator step (sign
   152	  the tarball locally; attach the `.asc` to the GitHub Release). It is not a
   153	  CI gate, there is no published npm signing key, and no release has shipped
   154	  one.
   155	- **Reproducible build** (`SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`-pinned `npm pack`) is specified
   156	  above but not yet asserted byte-for-byte in CI.
   157	- **SLSA Level-3** (hermetic build + two-party review) is out of scope; the
   158	  shipped provenance is SLSA Level-2 (`--provenance`).
   159	
   160	## References
   161	
   162	- PLAN-013 Phase E.7 (this ADR source)
   163	- PLAN-013 Phase 0 item 0.2 — `npm-publish.yml` RC + manual-approval gates
   164	- PLAN-177 W0 item 3 — the stale enforcement claim corrected to the
   165	  mechanism, and the whole-file sweep that followed it
   166	- ADR-040 §4 — credential lifecycle (90-day rotation applies to project keys)
   167	- `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` — publish pipeline (gated)
   168	- `.github/workflows/validate.yml` — packlist gate on `npm pack --dry-run`
   169	- `scripts/install-npm.sh` — local tarball build + `SHA256SUMS.txt` writer
   170	- `.claude/trust/owner.asc` — the Owner public key used for release-tag
   171	  verification (scope documented in `SECURITY.md`, not here)
   172	- `SECURITY.md` §How to verify what you install — the honest-limits statement
   173	  this file now mirrors
   174	- Sigstore + SLSA: <https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/levels>
   175	- npm provenance: <https://docs.npmjs.com/generating-provenance-statements>
